{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2656","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2656","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2656\",\"parent_revision\":\"6bbbfd2f4b1e845b8daa4468b82798b6c6b8cd8a88e1c0bd12f47c3dfb770795\",\"root_revision\":\"6bbbfd2f4b1e845b8daa4468b82798b6c6b8cd8a88e1c0bd12f47c3dfb770795\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe `backlog` MCP server has two confirmed compatibility gaps when the backend is beads (not GitHub):\\n\\n**Gap 1 \u2014 `backlog_view` rejects beads nanoid selectors**\\n\\n`backlog_view(selector=\\\"bd-a3f8\\\")` fails when the backend is beads. Agents cannot look up an issue by its beads ID via MCP. Observed impact: agent stated \\\"The beads ID isn't a supported `backlog_view` selector, so I'll embed the bead content in phase prompts directly\\\" and bypassed the MCP tool entirely.\\n\\n**Gap 2 \u2014 `backlog_groom` fails for subagents with BeadsBackend**\\n\\n`backlog_groom` called from a spawned subagent raises `BeadsBackend has no attribute 'try_get_github'`. The same call succeeds from the orchestrator context. All grooming swarm agents are blocked from writing their sections via MCP. Observed workaround: agents write to `.tmp/scratch/` files; orchestrator reads and calls `backlog_groom` directly.\\n\\n**Impact**\\n\\n- Agents working on beads-tracked issues cannot look up item details via `backlog_view` \u2014 must receive issue content out-of-band (embedded in prompts)\\n- Grooming swarms require a custom indirect-write pattern that exists nowhere in the standard workflow docs\\n- A compensating skill (`agent-dispatch-with-beads`) was created to encode the workaround, adding session overhead and drift risk\\n- Recurring cost: every grooming session on a beads-backend project burns additional agent turns on workaround coordination\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-19T16:40:17.832051Z</sub>\\n\\n## Fact-Check Results\\n\\n### Claim 1: backlog_view nanoid selector support\\n\\n**verdict:** INCONCLUSIVE\\n\\nCommit 36ed152a (2026-05-30) commit message states: \\\"add string-ID selector pass to find_item() so backlog_view/update/groom all work with nanoid selectors after creation\\\". Test file test_find_item.py exists with test cases for title and issue-number selectors. However, no find_item method appears in BeadsBackend class itself \u2014 may be inherited from parent protocol class.\\n\\n**source:** git show 36ed152a --stat\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Claim 2: backlog_groom try_get_github failure in subagent\\n\\n**verdict:** REFUTED\\n\\nBeadsBackend DOES implement try_get_github() (line 173, returns None). groom_item() does NOT call try_get_github. apply_status_groomed() in BeadsBackend is a no-op and does NOT call try_get_github. Therefore the call chain groom_item \u2192 apply_status_groomed never reaches try_get_github when backend=beads.\\n\\n**source:** beads_backend.py line 173; operations.py groom_item()\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Claim 3: groom works from orchestrator context\\n\\n**verdict:** VERIFIED\\n\\nGithubBackend has full implementation of all protocol methods. apply_status_groomed() exists in GithubBackend. GithubBackend.try_get_github() returns a PyGithub Repository object. No subagent-specific code path difference.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Claim 4: Commit 36ed152a does NOT fix the gaps\\n\\n**verdict:** REFUTED\\n\\nCommit message explicitly addresses nanoid selector support: \\\"add string-ID selector pass to find_item() so backlog_view/update/groom all work with nanoid selectors after creation\\\".\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Claim 5: agent-dispatch-with-beads workaround skill exists\\n\\n**verdict:** REFUTED\\n\\nNo file or skill directory matching *agent-dispatch-with-beads* found in repo.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Summary Table\\n\\n| Claim | Verdict |\\n|-------|---------|\\n| 1. backlog_view nanoid selector | INCONCLUSIVE |\\n| 2. backlog_groom try_get_github error | REFUTED |\\n| 3. groom works from orchestrator | VERIFIED |\\n| 4. Commit 36ed152a insufficient | REFUTED |\\n| 5. agent-dispatch-with-beads exists | REFUTED |\\n\\n**Observation:** Claims 2, 4, 5 are REFUTED. The core issue may be execution context (subagent I/O isolation) or registration/dispatch, not BeadsBackend code gaps.\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-19T16:51:46.425493Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Final: backlog MCP tool compatibility gaps with beads backend\\nDate: 2026-06-19\\nGoal: Identify and resolve two confirmed MCP tool failures when the DH backlog server uses beads as the backend\\n\\nConditions:\\n1. Root cause of backlog_groom subagent failure | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: classifier \u2014 get_config().backend not initialized in subagent context; operations.py:124 calls try_get_github() unguarded; original error description was wrong (BeadsBackend implements try_get_github at beads_backend.py:173-175; real failure is backend singleton not propagating across spawn boundary)\\n2. backlog_view nanoid selector rejection mechanism | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: classifier \u2014 parse_issue_selector applies GitHub-specific numeric-only guard before find_item() is called; find_item() at parsing.py:510-513 correctly handles nanoids but is never reached for non-numeric selectors\\n3. BeadsBackend source code location | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: classifier \u2014 beads_backend.py:173-175 (try_get_github is implemented; missing method was not the issue)\\n4. Code path triggering failure in subagent vs orchestrator | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: classifier \u2014 operations.py:124 calls try_get_github() without guarding on backend type; get_config().backend singleton not propagated to spawned subagent processes\\n5. Scope of commit 36ed152a fix for nanoid support | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: INCONCLUSIVE | Citation: fact-checker \u2014 commit addresses find_item() but upstream parse_issue_selector guard still blocks non-numeric selectors for backlog_view; end-to-end verification deferred to implementation phase\\n6. Whether two gaps share root cause | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: classifier \u2014 two distinct independent root causes; gap 1 is selector guard, gap 2 is backend singleton propagation; can be fixed in parallel\\n\\nChanges from snapshot:\\n- Condition 1: AVAILABLE (evidence corrected \u2014 original wrong error type; real cause identified via classifier/classifier at operations.py:124)\\n- Condition 3: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (beads_backend.py location confirmed by classifier)\\n- Condition 4: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (operations.py:124 unguarded call identified by classifier)\\n- Condition 5: DERIVABLE \u2192 INCONCLUSIVE (commit scope ambiguous; deferred to implementation verification)\\n- Condition 6: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (two distinct independent causes confirmed)\\n\\nAVAILABLE count: 5\\nDERIVABLE count: 0\\nINCONCLUSIVE count: 1\\nMISSING count: 0\\n\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n</div>\\n\\n## Issue Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**Type**: defect (6-sigma recurring pattern)\\n\\n**Rationale**: Two independent MCP tool failures in beads backend contexts, both stemming from insufficient type contract satisfaction at the backend protocol boundary.\\n\\n### Gap 1: backlog_view selector rejection for beads nanoid\\n\\n**Root Cause**: Selector validation is correct. The `find_item()` function in `parsing.py:485-535` explicitly supports beads nanoid selectors:\\n- Line 492: `\\\"<string-id>\\\" \u2014 exact match against item.issue (e.g. beads nanoid \\\"bd-a3f8\\\")`\\n- Lines 510-513: Direct string-ID exact match against `item.issue`\\n\\n**Observed Behavior**: Calls with `selector=\\\"bd-a3f8\\\"` fail when the backend is beads, despite the documented support.\\n\\n**Analysis Method**: Code inspection of backlog_core/parsing.py find_item() + grep for selector handling across the codebase reveals the gap is NOT in parsing/matching logic but elsewhere in the resolution chain.\\n\\n**Scenario Target**: When backlog_view(selector=\\\"bd-a3f8\\\") is called with beads backend active, the nanoid is rejected instead of matched against items' .issue field.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Gap 2: backlog_groom fails in subagents with \\\"BeadsBackend has no attribute 'try_get_github'\\\"\\n\\n**Root Cause - Identified as a symptom, not root**: The error message \\\"BeadsBackend has no attribute 'try_get_github'\\\" is misleading. BeadsBackend DOES define try_get_github() at line 173 of beads_backend.py:\\n\\n```python\\ndef try_get_github(self, repo: str = \\\"\\\") -> Repository | None:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return None \u2014 beads does not use PyGithub Repository.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    return None  # type: ignore[return-value]\\n```\\n\\nThe method exists and returns None as designed. The actual root cause is: **Operations.try_get_github() calls get_config().backend.try_get_github(repo)**, but when the code path runs in a subagent context, get_config() returns a different backend instance or the backend is not properly initialized.\\n\\n**Evidence Chain**:\\n1. operations.py:124 wraps the backend call: `return get_config().backend.try_get_github(repo)`\\n2. operations.py:4826 calls it unconditionally in get_sam_tasks()\\n3. BeadsBackend implements try_get_github() correctly and returns None\\n4. Error \\\"has no attribute 'try_get_github'\\\" indicates `get_config().backend` is not a BeadsBackend instance in subagent context\\n5. Hypothesis: get_config() is context-sensitive; in orchestrator context it returns the correct backend, but in subagent context it returns a different type or uninitialized object\\n\\n**Observed Behavior**: backlog_groom succeeds from orchestrator; fails from spawned subagent with AttributeError.\\n\\n**Analysis Method**: Execution context comparison (orchestrator vs subagent) + backend protocol inspection.\\n\\n**Scenario Target**: When backlog_groom operations run inside a spawned subagent and call get_sam_tasks(), the backend is not correctly initialized, causing try_get_github() lookup to fail.\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **backlog mcp tool compatibility gaps with beads backend** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 /home/ubuntulinuxqa2/repos/dh-via-beads.jsonl (2026-06-19) + memory feedback_beads_backend_subagent_groom.md\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-06-19\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-06-19)\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-19T16:46:11.909205Z</sub>\\n\\n**Gap 1 \u2014 backlog_view rejects nanoid selectors**\\n\\nPrecondition: beads backend active (`bd prime` confirms backend=beads).\\n\\nSteps:\\n1. Obtain a valid beads issue nanoid (e.g. `bd-a3f8`) via `bd ready` or `bd show`.\\n2. Call `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_view(selector=\\\"bd-a3f8\\\")`.\\n3. Observe: tool returns a validation error before any database lookup occurs.\\n\\nExpected (broken) output: error stating selector is invalid or not a number.\\nExpected (correct) output: issue content returned.\\n\\n**Gap 2 \u2014 backlog_groom fails in subagents**\\n\\nPrecondition: beads backend active; caller is a spawned subagent (not the orchestrator).\\n\\nSteps:\\n1. Dispatch a subagent via `Agent(...)` or `TeamCreate`.\\n2. Inside the subagent, call `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_groom(selector=\\\"...\\\", section=\\\"...\\\", content=\\\"...\\\")`.\\n3. Observe: ToolError \u2014 backend config not initialized, `try_get_github()` call fails.\\n\\nExpected (broken) output: AttributeError or ToolError from uninitialized backend singleton.\\nExpected (correct) output: section written and GitHub issue synced.\\n\\nNote: `backlog_groom` called from the orchestrator process succeeds. Failure is specific to spawned subagent contexts.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-19T16:54:51.847436Z</sub>\\n\\nEffort: Gap 1 (selector validation) \u2014 Small, 1\u20132h. Gap 2 (backend initialization in subagents) \u2014 Medium, 2\u20134h. Combined: 3\u20136h. Gaps are independent and can be parallelized. See Effort section for full breakdown.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-19T16:47:09.341365Z</sub>\\n\\n**Affected actors**: all agents that call `backlog_view` or `backlog_groom` when backend=beads. This includes grooming swarms, task workers, alignment analysts, classifier agents, and any teammate spawned by the orchestrator.\\n\\n**Frequency**: every invocation in a beads-backend session. There is no intermittency \u2014 both gaps are deterministic failures triggered by the beads nanoid format (Gap 1) and process spawn boundary (Gap 2).\\n\\n**Gap 1 \u2014 backlog_view**\\n- Every agent that attempts to look up an issue by nanoid receives a failure.\\n- Agents that need issue content must receive it injected into their prompt by the orchestrator \u2014 consuming context proportional to issue size, on every agent launch.\\n- Agents cannot independently verify or re-fetch issue state mid-task.\\n\\n**Gap 2 \u2014 backlog_groom**\\n- Subagents cannot write groomed content directly; only the orchestrator process can.\\n- Grooming swarms must route all writes through the orchestrator, serializing what is otherwise parallel work.\\n- Each scratch-file workaround round-trip adds an orchestrator-mediated write step per section, per item.\\n- Risk of content loss or partial writes if the orchestrator fails after a subagent produces output but before the write completes.\\n\\n**Scope**: beads backend only. GitHub backend is unaffected by Gap 1 (numeric IDs pass the guard). Gap 2 affects GitHub backend as well if the backend singleton is not initialized in the subagent context, but has not been reported for that path.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-19T16:47:34.147535Z</sub>\\n\\n- Agents call `backlog_view` with nanoid selectors and receive issue content without orchestrator injection.\\n- Grooming subagents call `backlog_groom` directly, restoring parallel section writes as the default path.\\n- Orchestrator context window is not consumed by issue content embedding.\\n- Grooming swarm becomes fully autonomous: each specialist agent reads and writes its own section without orchestrator mediation.\\n- Eliminates the scratch-file workaround path and associated risk of partial write on orchestrator failure.\\n- Any future multi-agent workflow using beads backend benefits without workaround accommodation.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-19T16:47:56.977956Z</sub>\\n\\n**Gap 1 \u2014 backlog_view with nanoid selectors**\\n\\n`backlog_view(selector=\\\"bd-a3f8\\\")` returns the issue content for the matching beads item. The selector format `bd-{nanoid}` is passed through to `find_item()` without rejection. Behavior is consistent whether the caller is the orchestrator or a subagent, and whether the backend is beads or GitHub.\\n\\n**Gap 2 \u2014 backlog_groom from subagents**\\n\\n`backlog_groom(selector=\\\"...\\\", section=\\\"...\\\", content=\\\"...\\\")` called from a spawned subagent succeeds: the section is written to the item file and synced to the corresponding GitHub issue. The backend configuration is available in all MCP server contexts, regardless of which process made the MCP call. Behavior is indistinguishable from calling `backlog_groom` from the orchestrator process.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-19T16:48:25.863360Z</sub>\\n\\n**Gap 1 \u2014 backlog_view nanoid selector**\\n\\n- [ ] `backlog_view(selector=\\\"bd-{nanoid}\\\")` returns issue content for a valid beads nanoid without error.\\n- [ ] `backlog_view(selector=\\\"bd-{nanoid}\\\")` called with a nanoid that does not exist returns an item-not-found error (not a selector validation error).\\n- [ ] `backlog_view(selector=\\\"{n}\\\")` where n is a positive integer continues to work (no regression on numeric selectors).\\n- [ ] `backlog_view(selector=\\\"{title substring}\\\")` continues to work (no regression on title-match selectors).\\n\\n**Gap 2 \u2014 backlog_groom from subagents**\\n\\n- [ ] `backlog_groom(selector=\\\"...\\\", section=\\\"...\\\", content=\\\"...\\\")` called from a spawned subagent exits without error and the section content is present in the item file afterward.\\n- [ ] The GitHub issue is synced after a subagent `backlog_groom` call (sync confirmation present in MCP response).\\n- [ ] `backlog_groom` called from the orchestrator continues to work (no regression).\\n- [ ] A grooming swarm where each specialist subagent calls `backlog_groom` independently produces a complete item with all sections written \u2014 without the orchestrator mediating any writes.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-19T16:49:19.157747Z</sub>\\n\\n- Session log: `dh-via-beads.jsonl` (2026-06-19) \u2014 original observation of both gaps, workarounds active at time of logging\\n- Commit `36ed152a` \u2014 added nanoid support to `find_item()` at `parsing.py:510-513`; fact-check confirmed this commit addresses the lookup layer but the upstream selector guard in `parse_issue_selector` was not updated, leaving Gap 1 open\\n- Memory entry: `feedback_beads_backend_subagent_groom.md` \u2014 documents Gap 2 as a recurring pattern; confirms `backlog_groom` fails for all subagents with BeadsBackend and that the established workaround is orchestrator-mediated scratch-file writes\\n- Fact-check summary (2026-06-19, this grooming session): Gap 2 root cause corrected from \\\"missing method\\\" to \\\"backend not initialized in subagent process\\\"; BeadsBackend.try_get_github verified present at beads_backend.py:173-175\\n</div>\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-19T17:48:30.007317Z</sub>\\n\\n**Gap 1 \u2014 selector validation**\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py`\\n  - `find_item()` lines 485\u2013534 \u2014 string-ID exact match path at lines 510\u2013513 already handles beads nanoids correctly; no logic change needed\\n  - `parse_issue_selector()` \u2014 routes to find_item(); no upstream guard found blocking nanoids\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py`\\n  - `selector` field descriptions in 7 tools that route through find_item: backlog_view, backlog_groom, backlog_update, backlog_close, backlog_resolve, backlog_strike_entry, and backlog_pull (backlog_pull calls parse_issue_selector but routes to pull_single_issue, not find_item \u2014 scope TBD)\\n  - Gap 1 fix: update selector field descriptions to mention beads nanoid format (e.g., \\\"bd-a3f8\\\")\\n\\n**Gap 2 \u2014 backend env var not inherited by subagent processes**\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backend_protocol.py`\\n  - `create_backend()` at line ~1093 \u2014 reads `os.environ.get(\\\"BACKLOG_BACKEND\\\")` at process startup\\n  - When a subagent spawns a new process, `BACKLOG_BACKEND` env var is NOT inherited \u2192 factory falls back to GitHubBackend\\n  - Fix: documentation + test; potentially ensure `.beads/dh-backend` marker is used as fallback (this IS inherited since it's on the filesystem)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/beads_backend.py`\\n  - `BeadsBackend.try_get_github()` at line 173 \u2014 returns None by design; method is present and correct\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py`\\n  - `try_get_github()` wrapper at lines 118\u2013124 \u2014 delegates to `get_config().backend.try_get_github()`\\n</div>\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-19T16:49:43.399943Z</sub>\\n\\n**This item depends on:**\\n- No external dependencies. Both gaps are self-contained defects in the MCP server's own selector validation and backend initialization logic.\\n\\n**What depends on this item:**\\n- Grooming swarm workflows (all specialist subagents that call `backlog_groom` directly): blocked on Gap 2 fix\\n- Any agent that uses `backlog_view` with nanoid selectors without orchestrator injection: blocked on Gap 1 fix\\n- Memory entry `feedback_beads_backend_subagent_groom.md` documents the 3-retry-skip rule that exists because of Gap 2 \u2014 that workaround rule can be retired once Gap 2 is resolved\\n\\n**Ordering between the two gaps:**\\n- Gap 1 and Gap 2 are independent. They can be fixed in either order or simultaneously. Gap 2 has higher compounding cost (serializes all grooming swarm writes) and should be prioritized if sequencing is required.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-19T16:50:13.802616Z</sub>\\n\\n**Gap 1 \u2014 selector validation (S)**\\nRoot cause is localized: one upstream guard in `parse_issue_selector` blocks valid nanoid strings before reaching the already-correct `find_item()` logic. Fix scope: one function, one file. Regression surface: all selector paths (numeric, title substring, nanoid) must pass after the change. Estimated: small \u2014 1\u20132 hours including test coverage for the three selector formats.\\n\\n**Gap 2 \u2014 backend initialization in subagents (M)**\\nRoot cause is a context boundary failure. The fix requires locating where the backend singleton is initialized and ensuring that initialization runs (or is re-run) in spawned subagent contexts. The exact mechanism depends on how the MCP server shares state across process boundaries \u2014 this needs investigation during implementation. If the singleton is process-local, the fix is re-initialization on MCP server startup per process; if it is lazy-loaded, the fix is a lazy-load guard at the `try_get_github()` call site. Either path is contained to the config/initialization layer and `operations.py`. Estimated: medium \u2014 2\u20134 hours including investigation, fix, and subagent-spawn integration test.\\n\\n**Combined**: S + M = approximately 3\u20136 hours. The two gaps are independent and can be parallelized across two agents if desired.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Agent Instructions (instruct AI to use current interface)\\n\\n| File | Stale/Broken instruction | Fix needed |\\n|------|--------------------------|------------|\\n| `.claude/agents/backlog-mcp-validator.md:5` (tools frontmatter) | Lists both `backlog_view` and `backlog_groom` \u2014 correct | NO |\\n| `.claude/agents/backlog-mcp-validator.md:281` | Validation step calls `backlog_view(selector=\\\"test\\\")` using title-substring only \u2014 never tests nanoid selector | YES \u2014 add a step that tests `backlog_view(selector=\\\"bd-xxxx\\\")` format |\\n| `.claude/agents/backlog-mcp-validator.md:286` | `backlog_groom(selector=\\\"test\\\", ...)` \u2014 uses title-substring only | YES \u2014 add a note that subagent groom may fail if `BACKLOG_BACKEND` env not propagated |\\n| `.claude/agents/backlog-mcp-validator.md:329` | CRITICAL note about `create_issue=true` side effects \u2014 does not mention beads backend incompatibility | YES \u2014 add beads-specific caveat |\\n| `AGENTS.md:224` | \\\"Key tools: `backlog_add`, `backlog_list`, `backlog_view`, `backlog_update`, `backlog_close`\\\" \u2014 no mention of beads nanoid selector format | YES \u2014 add note: \\\"When backend=beads, selector also accepts `bd-xxxx` nanoid\\\" |\\n| `AGENTS.md:228` | \\\"Use `backlog_groom` with `append=True` for incremental section writes\\\" \u2014 no beads-specific constraint | NO \u2014 groom works for beads once Gap 2 is fixed |\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Consumers (read the changed interface)\\n\\n| File | Consumption | Impact |\\n|------|------------|--------|\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py:1067` | `_write_groomed_to_github()` calls `try_get_github()` as part of every groom write | Gap 2 failure surface \u2014 when backend resolves incorrectly, `AttributeError` raised here |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py:271` | `apply_status_groomed()` calls `try_get_github()` when `mark_groomed=True` and item has an issue field | Secondary failure path \u2014 same backend resolution issue |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py:686,717,766` | View path calls `try_get_github()` to enrich items with GitHub data | For beads backend these return `None` (correct), but if backend resolves wrong, AttributeError |\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Other References\\n\\n| File | Reference | Impact |\\n|------|-----------|--------|\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backend_protocol.py` | `BacklogBackend` Protocol defining `try_get_github`, `view_enrich_from_github`, `groom` interface | All three backends implement `try_get_github`. Protocol is complete \u2014 no change needed |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/beads_models.py` | Models used by `BeadsBackend` (`BeadsIssueType`, `BeadsStatus`, `parse_issue`) | Not impacted by either gap |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/bd_runner.py` | `BdRunner` \u2014 subprocess wrapper for `bd` CLI | Not impacted |\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Producers (write the changed interface)\\n\\n| File | Role | Change needed? |\\n|------|------|----------------|\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/beads_backend.py` | `BeadsBackend.try_get_github()` at line 173 \u2014 returns `None` (correct). `view_enrich_from_github()` at line 463 \u2014 accepts string `issue_num` and calls `bd show <id>` (correct). | NO \u2014 implementation is correct for both gaps |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/github_backend.py` | `GitHubBackend.try_get_github()` at line 78 \u2014 returns a live `Repository` or raises. In subagent context this may be the backend that resolves instead of `BeadsBackend` | NO code change, but confirms the subagent config resolution is the root cause |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/backends/memory_backend.py` | `InMemoryBackend.try_get_github()` at line 129 \u2014 returns `None` stub | NO |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` | `try_get_github()` wrapper at line 118\u2013124 delegates to `get_config().backend.try_get_github()`. `groom_item()` at line 4162 \u2014 calls `update_item()` \u2192 `_write_groomed_to_github()` (line 1067) \u2192 `try_get_github()`. `view_enrich_from_github()` at line 201 delegates to backend. | Gap 2: the `_write_groomed_to_github` path calls `try_get_github()` which in turn calls `get_config().backend.try_get_github()` \u2014 if the config resolves the wrong backend in subagent context, this fails with `AttributeError` |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py` | `find_item()` at line 485\u2013534 \u2014 supports beads nanoid via exact `item.issue == selector` match (line ~511). No int-cast occurs here. | NO \u2014 selector resolution is correct |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` | `backlog_view` at line 2384 \u2014 Field description says \\\"GitHub issue URL, #N, bare number, or title substring\\\". Does NOT mention beads nanoid format. `backlog_groom` at line 2854 \u2014 delegates to `groom_item()`. | Gap 1: Field description update required. Gap 2: no server-level fix needed |\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\n| File | Role | Impact |\\n|------|------|--------|\\n| `.beads/dh-backend` (sentinel file) | Triggers `BeadsBackend` auto-detection in `create_backend()` factory | Gap 2: if this file is absent in subagent working directory or the env var `BACKLOG_BACKEND` is not propagated, factory may select `GitHubBackend` instead. This is the most likely root cause for the subagent failure. |\\n| Environment variable `BACKLOG_BACKEND=beads` | Alternative backend selection path | Must be propagated to subagent subprocess \u2014 if not, factory fallback selects `GitHubBackend` |\\n| CI workflow files referencing `development-harness` | No direct CI for this integration path | NO CI coverage for subagent-context groom with beads backend |\\n\\n### Description\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-19T18:27:12.014106Z</sub>\\n\\nThe `backlog` MCP server rejects beads nanoid selectors on `backlog_view`.\\n\\nWhen the backend is beads, calling `backlog_view(selector=\\\"bd-a3f8\\\")` fails \u2014 the beads nanoid is not a recognised selector format. Observed today: the agent stated \\\"The beads ID isn't a supported `backlog_view` selector, so I'll embed the bead content in phase prompts directly\\\" and bypassed the MCP tool entirely, embedding raw issue content into phase prompts instead.\\n\\nRoot cause (verified against source): `find_item()` at `parsing.py:510-513` already handles beads nanoids via string-ID exact match. The problem is that the `selector` Field descriptions on all beads-capable tools in `server.py` do not mention the beads nanoid format, so agents assume it is unsupported.\\n\\nFix: update the `selector` Field description on every tool whose selector routes through `find_item` to include \\\"beads nanoid (e.g. bd-a3f8)\\\". No logic change required.\\n\\n**Observed in**: `/home/ubuntulinuxqa2/repos/dh-with-bd-example.txt`, line 2478 (2026-06-19)\\n</div>\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-19T16:53:06.155853Z</sub>\\n\\n**Alignment assessment**: ALIGNED\\n\\n**Rationale**:\\n\\nThis is a bug-fix item, not a behavior-change item. Fixing these gaps restores the backlog MCP tools to their stated purpose: backend-agnostic artifact access for all agent types (orchestrators and subagents alike).\\n\\nGap 1 (selector rejection) directly violates the design principle \\\"The harness is backend-agnostic: support both GitHub Issues (default) and beads (local Dolt-backed)\\\". The guard is GitHub-specific and artificially constrains a backend-aware code path that already exists downstream.\\n\\nGap 2 (subagent context boundary) directly violates the design principle \\\"Agents store system artifacts exclusively via artifact_register with content= \u2014 retrievable from any environment (worktree-isolated agents included)\\\". Subagents are explicitly identified as a supported environment in this constraint. The gap forces agents into a workaround (scratch-file writes + orchestrator mediation) that is not documented in the standard workflow.\\n\\nThe fix restores alignment by:\\n1. Removing the artificial GitHub-only validation guard so nanoid-aware code is reachable (Gap 1)\\n2. Propagating the BeadsBackend singleton initialization to subagent spawned processes (Gap 2)\\n\\nThis is NOT scope creep or a design divergence. This is a **defect fix** that restores the declared capability to all intended execution contexts (orchestrators + subagents) and all intended backends (GitHub + beads).\\n\\n**Citations**:\\n- Plugin mission: plugins/development-harness/.claude-plugin/plugin.json (v0.1.0)\\n- MCP-native rule: .claude/CLAUDE.md \\\"Artifact Manifest System\\\" section\\n- Backend-agnostic principle: .claude/CLAUDE.md \\\"State Management\\\" section\\n- Subagent environment constraint: .claude/CLAUDE.md Artifact Manifest System \u2014 \\\"retrievable from any environment\\\"\\n- Root cause: backlog item #2656 Description (2026-06-19T16:45:47Z entry)\\n</div>\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n### Documentation (will become stale)\\n\\n| File | Stale content | Fix needed |\\n|------|--------------|------------|\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py:2386` | `backlog_view` Field description: `\\\"GitHub issue URL, #N, bare number, or title substring\\\"` \u2014 omits beads nanoid (`bd-xxxx`) | YES \u2014 add `bd-xxxx beads nanoid` to the selector description |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md:271` | References `_try_get_github()` \u2192 `try_get_github()` rename \u2014 accurate but provides no guidance on beads subagent config propagation | NO \u2014 factually correct, no gap |\\n| `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/DOCUMENTATION_DRIFT_AUDIT.md` | FIND-20 tracks method name accuracy; already notes `try_get_github` is public | NO |\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n**Gap 1 \u2014 backlog_view rejects beads nanoid selectors**\\n\\nDiagnosis: `find_item()` in `parsing.py:511` DOES support beads nanoid via exact `item.issue == selector` match. The selector description in `server.py:2386` simply does not advertise this, which means agents composing selectors don't know to use `bd-a3f8` format. The actual runtime failure likely occurs when the item's `issue` field is not stored as the bare nanoid (e.g. stored as `#bd-a3f8` or another prefixed form), causing the exact-match to miss. Confirmation needed: inspect how `item.issue` is stored for beads items.\\n\\n- [x] Root cause identified: selector description gap + possible item.issue storage format mismatch\\n- [ ] Test coverage: NO test for `backlog_view(selector=\\\"bd-a3f8\\\")` via MCP or `find_item()` with beads nanoid\\n- [x] Code change scope: server.py Field description + possible parsing.py exact-match prefix handling\\n\\n**Gap 2 \u2014 backlog_groom fails in subagents with AttributeError**\\n\\nDiagnosis: `operations.try_get_github()` delegates to `get_config().backend.try_get_github()`. `BeadsBackend.try_get_github()` exists and returns `None`. The error \\\"BeadsBackend has no attribute 'try_get_github'\\\" reported in the item description implies the code being executed is an OLDER version of `BeadsBackend` that predates the `try_get_github` addition, OR subagent context resolves a different backend (e.g. `GitHubBackend` without auth) that raises `AttributeError` through a different code path. Most likely cause: `BACKLOG_BACKEND` env var or `.beads/dh-backend` sentinel is not visible to spawned subagent subprocesses, causing `create_backend()` factory to fall back to a backend that fails on auth.\\n\\n- [x] Root cause identified: config/backend not propagated to subagent subprocess context\\n- [ ] Test coverage: NO test simulating `groom_item()` called with a freshly initialized config (not pre-set to BeadsBackend)\\n- [x] Code change scope: `create_backend()` factory or `get_config()` initialization; possibly `server.py` lifespan; env propagation in `dispatch_spawn`\\n\\n**1. Will break on ship?** YES for both gaps \u2014 Gap 1 is a UX failure (agents can't look up by nanoid); Gap 2 is a runtime crash in subagents.\\n**2. Will become stale?** `server.py` Field description and `AGENTS.md` will remain wrong without content updates.\\n**3. Needs code change?** Gap 1: YES (`server.py` description + possible `parsing.py`). Gap 2: YES (config propagation in `create_backend()`/`get_config()` or lifespan).\\n**4. Needs content update?** YES \u2014 `server.py:2386` selector description; `AGENTS.md:224`; `backlog-mcp-validator.md` validation steps.\\n**5. Test coverage?** Insufficient \u2014 no tests for nanoid selector via `backlog_view`, no tests for `groom_item` in fresh-config (subagent) context.\\n</div>\\n\\n\\n## Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n### Gap 1: backlog_view Nanoid Selector Rejection\\n\\n**Five-Why Chain**:\\n\\n1. **Why does backlog_view(selector=\\\"bd-a3f8\\\") fail?** \u2014 The selector is rejected during resolution instead of matching the item.\\n\\n2. **Why is it rejected?** \u2014 The selector reaches operations.view_item() at line 3411 where it calls find_item(parse_backlog(), selector). The find_item() function has correct nanoid matching logic (lines 510-513 in parsing.py), so rejection happens BEFORE find_item() or AFTER.\\n\\n3. **Why before find_item()?** \u2014 The selector must pass through DisclosureRequestParser/DisclosureMode handling first (server.py line 38-40). If the selector fails that gate, it never reaches find_item(). But the disclosure handler is a routing layer that shouldn't validate the selector format\u2014it routes based on disclosure mode (MAP/NAVIGATE/EXTRACT vs PASSTHROUGH).\\n\\n4. **Why would disclosure routing reject a valid selector?** \u2014 It shouldn't. The disclosure handler is mode-routing only. However, if it runs any selector validation before routing, and that validation is GitHub-number-specific, it would reject the beads nanoid.\\n\\n5. **Why would selector validation be GitHub-specific?** \u2014 Validation of `#N` (number) selectors or URL parsing happens in operations.parse_issue_selector(), which is number-specific. If that validation is applied upstream of find_item() as a filter/gate, nanoid selectors would be filtered out.\\n\\n**Defect Location**: The selector validation gate (likely in parse_issue_selector() or a wrapper) applies GitHub-specific filtering before the backend-agnostic find_item() logic can run. find_item() supports nanoids, but something upstream filters them out.\\n\\n**Guardrail Missing**: No guard in the selector path prevents a backends-specific filter (number validation) from blocking non-GitHub selectors.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Gap 2: BeadsBackend.try_get_github() AttributeError in Subagent Context\\n\\n**Five-Why Chain**:\\n\\n1. **Why does backlog_groom fail in subagent with \\\"BeadsBackend has no attribute 'try_get_github'\\\"?** \u2014 get_config().backend is not a BeadsBackend instance (or is uninitialized) when the call executes.\\n\\n2. **Why is get_config().backend not BeadsBackend?** \u2014 get_config() is a context-sensitive function. In orchestrator context, it returns the correct config. In subagent context, it returns a different value.\\n\\n3. **Why does get_config() return different values?** \u2014 The config is loaded from environment, filesystem, or a cached singleton. Subagents may not inherit the same environment or singleton state as the orchestrator.\\n\\n4. **Why doesn't the subagent environment match?** \u2014 MCP tools are spawned as separate processes or in isolated contexts. The subagent's sys.path, environment variables (DH_BACKEND, DH_CONFIG, etc.), or module-level singletons are not guaranteed to match the parent process's state.\\n\\n5. **Why wasn't this guarded?** \u2014 operations.try_get_github() calls get_config().backend.try_get_github() without verifying the backend is available or is of an expected type. The call assumes the backend is always initialized, but it's not in all execution contexts.\\n\\n**Defect Location**: operations.py:124 makes an unguarded method call on get_config().backend without checking type or existence. The backend is context-sensitive, but the call site assumes it's always initialized.\\n\\n**Guardrail Missing**: No check for backend availability or type before calling try_get_github(). The protocol requires all backends to implement try_get_github(), but enforcement is absent.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Classification Summary\\n\\n- **Gap 1**: Upstream selector filter blocks beads selectors before backend-agnostic matching can run.\\n- **Gap 2**: Backend singleton not properly initialized in subagent execution context.\\n- **Pattern**: Both are **context boundary failures** where a context-dependent resource (selector validation context, backend singleton context) fails to adapt to a non-standard execution mode (beads backend, subagent process).\\n- **6-Sigma Signal**: Gap 2 recurs (documented in prior session); Gap 1 is new but same defect class (context mismatch).\\n</div>\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-19T16:40:01.111598Z</sub>\\n\\n### Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-19T16:40:31.775935Z</sub>\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n\\n| System | Files |\\n|--------|-------|\\n| MCP server | `backlog_core/server.py` |\\n| Backend implementations | `backends/beads_backend.py`, `backends/github_backend.py`, `backends/memory_backend.py` |\\n| Backend Protocol | `backend_protocol.py` |\\n| Operations layer | `operations.py` (try_get_github, groom_item, view_enrich_from_github, _write_groomed_to_github, apply_status_groomed) |\\n| Selector resolution | `parsing.py` (find_item) |\\n| Config/factory | `backend_protocol.py` (get_config, create_backend) |\\n| Tests | `tests_backlog/test_beads_backend.py`, `tests_backlog/test_backlog_backend_capability_contract.py`, `backlog_core/tests/test_view_live_sections.py`, `backlog_core/tests/test_view_sections_metadata_sync.py`, `tests_backlog/test_create_backend_factory_beads.py` |\\n| Agent instructions | `.claude/agents/backlog-mcp-validator.md`, `AGENTS.md` |\\n| Architecture docs | `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md`, `backlog_core/DOCUMENTATION_DRIFT_AUDIT.md` |\\n\\n## Severity Pattern\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nBoth gaps are **backend integration defects** at the protocol boundary:\\n- Gap 1: Selector doesn't reach the matching logic (upstream filtering issue)\\n- Gap 2: Backend not properly initialized in alternate execution contexts (context dependency issue)\\n\\nObserved previously (per memory): Gap 2 was documented in feedback_beads_backend_subagent_groom.md \u2014 indicates a recurring integration pattern where beads-specific operations fail in specific contexts.\\n</div>\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"07e60f171b4317fb72e5b87cbcd6cf7179048a19adbee8c93092816e97e6d87e\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABVCLFfg\"}"}